  <h1>How to Evaluate an IVD Manufacturer</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> Evaluate an IVD manufacturer on four things — <strong>regulatory status</strong> (ISO 13485, CE, FDA listing, NMPA), <strong>product performance</strong> (sensitivity, specificity, datasheet), <strong>manufacturing quality</strong> (QC, validation, traceability), and <strong>documentation</strong> (COA, IFU, certificates). A supplier who can show the lot-specific evidence for each is the one that has de-risked your order.</p>

  <h2>The four evaluation dimensions</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Regulatory status</strong> — ISO 13485 quality system; CE marking (with the conformity procedure and authorized representative); FDA establishment registration / device listing (not confused with 510(k) clearance); NMPA registration for China manufacture.</li>
    <li><strong>Product performance</strong> — analytical sensitivity, specificity, LoD and cut-off from the datasheet or validation report, not a marketing claim.</li>
    <li><strong>Manufacturing quality</strong> — QC per lot, batch release, raw-material traceability, stability data, retained samples.</li>
    <li><strong>Documentation</strong> — a lot-specific COA, IFU, and certificates in the supplier's own name.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Qualification checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Certificate: current, in the supplier's name, covering the products you buy.</li>
    <li>COA: lot-specific, showing measured values — not a range copied from a catalogue.</li>
    <li>Sensitivity / specificity / LoD: stated in the datasheet for the specific analyte.</li>
    <li>Traceability: from raw material to finished lot, with retained samples.</li>
    <li>CE conformity procedure: Annex III self-declaration vs Notified Body — know the difference.</li>
    <li>FDA wording: "device listing" is not "510(k) cleared" — check which one is claimed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Red flags that signal risk</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No lot-specific COA — only a generic certificate or catalogue range.</li>
    <li>"FDA on request" without a K-number or a clear listing statement.</li>
    <li>No traceability from raw material to finished lot.</li>
    <li>A certificate in a different company's name (broker reselling someone else's cert).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
  <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the single most useful test of an IVD supplier is whether they can produce a lot-specific COA and the performance datasheet for the analyte you are buying — not just a certificate wall.</p>
  <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> A certificate proves the quality system; the COA and datasheet prove the product. An IVD buyer who accepts a certificate without the lot-level evidence has not reduced their regulatory or performance risk — they have described it. Ask for the number, not the logo.</p>
  <p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as an IVD manufacturer's regulatory team, not a contractual or legal statement.</p>

